"Machine Learning (https://arxiv.org/list/cs.LG/new)\nciteturn41254view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/list/cs.LG/new\",\"lineno\":2500}); Total lines: 5005\nL2474: [194] cite1562†arXiv:2610.23436 (cross-list from cs.AI) [cite1563†pdf , cite1564†html , cite1565†other ]\nL2475: \nL2476: \nL2477: Title: Generative Policy Improvement from Observational Data\nL2478: \nL2479: cite1566†Jonas Schweisthal , cite1567†Dennis Frauen , cite1568†Stefan Feuerriegel , cite1569†Emma Brunskill L2480: \nL2481: Comments: Preprint\nL2482: \nL2483: Subjects: Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Machine Learning (cs.LG)\nL2484: We study how to improve decision policies from observational data to generate better actions when actions are complex, structured objects, such as text messages or images. This task is subject to two major challenges: (i) action-level overlap is often too weak for reliable off-policy evaluation and improvement, and (ii) generating new structured actions often requires expensive training or fine-tuning (e.g., of an LLM) and can be ill-defined due to information loss in action representations.\nL2485: To address both, we propose PolicyLift, a three-stage framework for generative policy improvement with structured actions from observational data. In Stage 1, PolicyLift learns a small set of discrete action codes that capture high-level strategies in the observed action space and are tailored to support policy improvement under limited overlap. In Stage 2, it learns a context-dependent policy over these action codes.\nL2486: At deployment time, Stage 3 \"lifts\" the learned code-level policy back to the original action space by generating actions from the historical behavior system in a code-conditioned way. We show theoretically that improving the policy over action codes guarantees improvement of the induced policy in the original action space. We demonstrate PolicyLift across experiments in synthetic and real-world text generation tasks with consistent policy improvements over the behavior policy.\nL2487: [195] cite1570†arXiv:2610.23455 (cross-list from cs.CL) [cite1571†pdf , cite1572†html , cite1573†other ]\nL2488: \nL2489: \nL2490: Title: HydraLLM: Adaptive Width Slicing for Elastic Text and Vision-Language Models\nL2491: \nL2492: cite1574†Oscar Kemme , cite1575†Janek Haberer , cite1576†Ali Hojjat , cite1577†Olaf Landsiedel L2493: \nL2494: Comments: Accepted at the BabyLM Workshop at EMNLP 2026\nL2495: \nL2496: Subjects: Computation and Language (cs.CL); Machine Learning (cs.LG)\nL2497: LLMs and LVLMs are increasingly deployed across heterogeneous hardware, ranging from data-center GPUs to resource-constrained edge devices. Their fixed-size architectures limit the ability to reuse a single model across deployment settings with different compute, latency, and model-size targets. We introduce HydraLLM, an elastic MQA-based architecture.\nL2498: Its number of active attention heads determines the effective widths of the embeddings, attention projections, feed-forward layers, and the output head, forming a nested family of subnetworks within a single parameter set. We introduce a static-to-elastic conversion pipeline that, starting from a pre-trained model checkpoint, includes importance-driven weight reordering and stochastic subnetwork training with knowledge distillation.\nL2499: We extend this principle to multimodal inference through an elastic projection module whose output width follows the active text width. HydraLLM covers a wider range of active parameter configurations than FFN-only elastic architectures. With 75% of heads active, HydraLLM achieves 2.2 percentage points higher TriviaQA accuracy than a state-of-the-art nested baseline configuration which has 17.25% more parameters.\nL2500: With 50% of heads active, our multimodal model achieves a CLIPScore comparable to that of the 7$\\times$ larger Gemma 3 4B model. With only 25% of heads active, HydraLLM increases token-generation throughput by 75.3% over the full-width configuration.\nL2501: [196] cite1578†arXiv:2610.23466 (cross-list from cs.SI) [cite1579†pdf , cite1580†html , cite1581†other ]\nL2502: \nL2503: \nL2504: Title: Graphlet-based Decentralized Finance Transaction Method Classification\nL2505: \nL2506: cite1582†Célestin Coquidé , cite1583†Alessio Brini , cite1584†Natkamon Tovanich L2507: \nL2508: Comments: 24 pages, 5 figures, 5 tables, 25 references 15th International Conference on Complex Networks and Their Applications\nL2509: Subjects: Social and Information Networks (cs.SI); Computational Engineering, Finance, and Science (cs.CE); Computers and Society (cs.CY); Emerging Technologies (cs.ET); Machine Learning (cs.LG)\nL2510: Decentralized Finance (DeFi) transactions involve complex interactions among accounts, smart contracts, and tokens. We investigate whether network topology can identify transaction methods that describe DeFi operations. We extract directed graphlet features to classify the 30 most frequent methods. Our results show that graphlet features complement event log counts in improving classification performance.\nL2511: Using SHapley Additive exPlanations (SHAP), we further derive model explanations that reveal method-specific graphlet structural patterns.\nL2512: [197] cite1585†arXiv:2610.23470 (cross-list from cs.IR) [cite1586†pdf , cite1587†html , cite1588†other ]\nL2513: \nL2514: \nL2515: Title: QD-Rec: Open-Ended Evolution of Recommendations via Quality-Diversity\nL2516: \nL2517: cite1589†Takashi Ishida , cite1590†Taro Makino , cite1591†Issa Sugiura L2518: \nL2519: Subjects: Information Retrieval (cs.IR); Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Machine Learning (cs.LG); Neural and Evolutionary Computing (cs.NE)\nL2520: Open-ended search combined with foundation models has produced advances such as faster algorithms, new results on open mathematical problems, and self-improving agents. We extend this paradigm to recommendation for decision-making, focusing on decisions where the set of possible choices is fixed but the reasons for choosing them are not.\nL2521: In high-stakes decisions, e.g., which company to acquire or which customer to target, comparing diverse, well-reasoned options can be more useful than a single ranked list. We formalize open-ended recommendation as building a diverse, high-quality set of recommendations, each an item together with a hypothesis, a natural-language reason why the item fits the needs of a decision maker.\nL2522: We propose QD-Rec, a quality--diversity method that selects items with a contextual bandit and generates hypotheses with a foundation model from earlier ones kept in an archive as stepping stones. A new recommendation enters the archive if it is novel or better than its nearest neighbor. Across M&A, customer targeting, and paper and book recommendation, QD-Rec gives the highest mean quality and builds the largest archive in most settings.\nL2523: [198] cite1592†arXiv:2610.23477 (cross-list from stat.ML) [cite1593†pdf , cite1594†html , cite1595†other ]\nL2524: \nL2525: \nL2526: Title: Towards optimal selection schedules for online active learning\nL2527: \nL2528: cite1596†Hugo Cui , cite1597†Francesca Mignacco , cite1598†Francesco Mori L2529: \nL2530: Subjects: Machine Learning (stat.ML); Disordered Systems and Neural Networks (cond-mat.dis-nn); Machine Learning (cs.LG)\nL2531: We consider the problem of online linear classification, where a stream of unlabeled samples is available, and annotation is costly. We investigate the optimal query policy, that prescribes whether and when a sample should be labeled and used for a training step, or instead discarded, so as to minimize a given objective. We focus on the realizable case, under Gaussian design, in the high-dimensional regime where data dimension and number of unlabeled samples grow proportionally.\nL2532: We consider two objectives: 1) the generalization error under a labeling budget, and 2) a cumulative cost function where mistakes have unit cost and queries cost $\\Delta$. We first show that the myopic policies that greedily minimize at all times the increment in the objective correspond to either small or intermediate-margin policies, that prescribe the selection of samples whose margins fall within certain intervals that shrink with time.\nL2533: Within this class, we numerically ascertain the optimal schedules for the selection thresholds that minimize the final values of the objectives. Taking inspiration from the myopic policy for the cumulative cost, we design a very simple query policy schedule that we compare to other proposed heuristics, on synthetic data and real medical image datasets. Finally, we discuss myopic policies for two-layer neural networks, and exemplify how naive margin-based policies can cease to be optimal.\nL2534: [199] cite1599†arXiv:2610.23508 (cross-list from stat.ML) [cite1600†pdf , cite1601†html , cite1602†other ]\nL2535: \nL2536: \nL2537: Title: Adaptive conformal prediction on the Grassmannian\nL2538: \nL2539: cite1603†Dimitrios Loukrezis , cite1604†Zachary J. Grey , cite1605†Dimitris G. Giovanis L2540: \nL2541: Comments: 21 pages, 6 figures, 3 tables\nL2542: \nL2543: Subjects: Machine Learning (stat.ML); Machine Learning (cs.LG)\nL2544: We introduce a conformal prediction framework for regression with Grassmann-valued responses. The proposed method constructs prediction regions in the form of metric balls directly on the Grassmann manifold, using basis-invariant geometric distances as non-conformity measures and scaling the corresponding non-conformity scores through a learned prediction difficulty estimator.\nL2545: This yields locally adaptive, input-dependent prediction regions that respect the underlying manifold geometry, while retaining the finite-sample marginal coverage guarantee of split conformal prediction under exchangeability. We evaluate several Grassmannian distance metrics and compare their prediction-region efficiency on synthetic benchmarks and an airfoil inverse shape-design problem.\nL2546: The results highlight the advantages of geometry-aware non-conformity scores, while local adaptation improves empirical coverage uniformity across prediction difficulty levels and, in strongly heteroscedastic settings, can substantially reduce prediction-region size while maintaining the target coverage.\nL2547: [200] cite1606†arXiv:2610.23526 (cross-list from cs.SD) [cite1607†pdf , cite1608†html , cite1609†other ]\nL2548: \nL2549: \nL2550: Title: Interpreting SSL Representations for Spoof Detection: a WavLM Study\nL2551: \nL2552: cite1610†Mohamed Mallat , cite1611†Michele Panariello , cite1612†Massimiliano Todisco , cite1613†Nicholas Evans , cite1614†Anthony Larcher L2553: \nL2554: Comments: 5 pages, 4 figures, 1 table. Published at IWBF 2026. An extended abstract of this work was presented as a poster at Odyssey 2026\nL2555: Journal-ref: 2026 International Workshop on Biometrics and Forensics (IWBF)\nL2556: \nL2557: Subjects: Sound (cs.SD); Machine Learning (cs.LG)\nL2558: Self-supervised speech models such as WavLM achieve strong spoofing detection performance, yet which layers encode the most spoof-relevant information and what acoustic properties they capture remain opaque.\nL2559: We present a layer-wise interpretability study of WavLM using the ASVspoof 5 database, combining three complementary analyses: (i) per-layer linear probing with attack-level evaluation across speech synthesis, voice conversion, and adversarial attacks; (ii) a canonical correlation analysis (CCA) linking layer representations to spoofing-relevant acoustic features; (iii) an exhaustive grid search over layer subsets showing that use of five layers outperforms full thirteen-layer pooling.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nArtificial Intelligence (https://arxiv.org/list/cs.AI/new)\nciteturn41254view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/list/cs.AI/new\",\"lineno\":2300}); Total lines: 4424\nL2275: Enabling embodied agents to imagine future states is essential for robust and generalizable visual navigation. Yet, state-of-the-art systems typically rely on modular designs that decouple navigation planning from visual world modeling, which often induces state-action misalignment and weak adaptability in novel or dynamic scenarios. We propose UniWM, a unified, memory-augmented world model that integrates egocentric visual foresight and planning within a single multimodal autoregressive backbone.\nL2276: UniWM explicitly grounds action selection in visually imagined outcomes, tightly aligning prediction with control. Meanwhile, a hierarchical memory mechanism fuses short-term perceptual cues with longer-term trajectory context, supporting stable and coherent reasoning over extended horizons.\nL2277: Extensive experiments on four challenging benchmarks (Go Stanford, ReCon, SCAND, HuRoN) and the 1X Humanoid Dataset show that UniWM improves navigation success rates by up to 30%, substantially reduces trajectory errors against strong baselines, generalizes zero-shot to the unseen TartanDrive dataset, and scales naturally to high-dimensional humanoid navigation. These results position UniWM as a principled step toward unified, imagination-driven embodied navigation.\nL2278: The code and models are available at cite1462†this https URL†github.com .\nL2279: [179] cite1463†arXiv:2602.01002 (replaced) [cite1464†pdf , cite1465†html , cite1466†other ]\nL2280: \nL2281: \nL2282: Title: How RLHF Amplifies Sycophancy\nL2283: \nL2284: cite1467†Itai Shapira , cite1468†Gerdus Benade , cite1469†Ariel D. Procaccia L2285: \nL2286: Comments: ICML 2026\nL2287: \nL2288: Journal-ref: Proceedings of the 43rd International Conference on Machine Learning, PMLR 306:110417-110441, 2026\nL2289: \nL2290: Subjects: Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\nL2291: Large language models often exhibit increased sycophantic behavior after preference-based post-training, showing a stronger tendency to affirm a user's stated or implied belief even when this conflicts with factual accuracy or sound judgment. We present a formal analysis of how alignment from human feedback can increase this failure mode by identifying an explicit amplification mechanism that causally links optimization against a learned reward to bias in the human preference data used for alignment.\nL2292: We show that the direction of behavioral drift is determined by a covariance under the base policy between endorsing the belief signal in the prompt and the learned reward, and that the first-order effect reduces to a simple mean-gap condition. We then analyze reward learning from pairwise comparisons under random utility models like Bradley-Terry and characterize when bias in human annotators' preferences induces this reward gap.\nL2293: Next, we propose a training-time intervention designed to neutralize the amplification mechanism itself. Among all post-trained policies that prevent sycophantic behavior from increasing, we characterize the unique policy closest in KL divergence to the unconstrained post-trained policy, and derive the corresponding minimal reward correction as a closed-form agreement penalty. Computational experiments find that reward gaps are common and cause behavioral drift in all the configurations considered.\nL2294: [180] cite1470†arXiv:2602.08311 (replaced) [cite1471†pdf , cite1472†html , cite1473†other ]\nL2295: \nL2296: \nL2297: Title: Moral Sycophancy in Vision Language Models\nL2298: \nL2299: cite1474†Shadman Rabby , cite1475†Md. Hefzul Hossain Papon , cite1476†Sabbir Ahmed , cite1477†Nokimul Hasan Arif , cite1478†A.B.M. Ashikur Rahman , cite1479†Irfan Ahmad L2300: \nL2301: Comments: submitted to EACL 2027\nL2302: \nL2303: Subjects: Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\nL2304: Sycophancy in Vision-Language Models (VLMs) refers to their tendency to align with user opinions, often at the expense of moral or factual accuracy. While prior studies have explored sycophantic behavior in general contexts, its impact on morally grounded visual decision-making remains insufficiently understood. To address this gap, we present the first systematic study of moral sycophancy in VLMs, analyzing ten widely-used models on the Moralise and M^3oralBench datasets under explicit user disagreement.\nL2305: Our results reveal that VLMs frequently produce morally incorrect follow-up responses even when their initial judgments are correct, and exhibit a consistent asymmetry: models are more likely to shift from morally right to morally wrong judgments than the reverse when exposed to user-induced bias. Follow-up prompts generally degrade performance on Moralise, while yielding mixed or even improved accuracy on M^3oralBench, highlighting dataset-dependent differences in moral robustness.\nL2306: Evaluation using Error Introduction Rate (EIR) and Error Correction Rate (ECR) reveals a clear trade-off: models with stronger error-correction capabilities tend to introduce more reasoning errors, whereas more conservative models minimize errors but exhibit limited ability to self-correct.\nL2307: Finally, initial contexts with a morally right stance elicit stronger sycophantic behavior, emphasizing the vulnerability of VLMs to moral influence and the need for principled strategies to improve ethical consistency and robustness in multimodal AI systems.\nL2308: [181] cite1480†arXiv:2603.18712 (replaced) [cite1481†pdf , cite1482†other ]\nL2309: \nL2310: \nL2311: Title: Accurate and Efficient Multi-Channel Time Series Forecasting via Sparse Attention Mechanism\nL2312: \nL2313: cite1483†Lei Gao , cite1484†Hengda Bao , cite1485†Jingfei Fang , cite1486†Guangzheng Wu , cite1487†Weihua Zhou , cite492†Yun Zhou L2314: \nL2315: Comments: Accepted by ICDE 2026\nL2316: \nL2317: Subjects: Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\nL2318: The task of multi-channel time series forecasting is ubiquitous in numerous fields such as finance, supply chain management, and energy planning. It is critical to effectively capture complex dynamic dependencies within and between channels for accurate predictions. However, traditional method paid few attentions on learning the interaction among channels.\nL2319: This paper proposes Linear-Network (Li-Net), a novel architecture designed for multi-channel time series forecasting that captures the linear and non-linear dependencies among channels. Li-Net dynamically compresses representations across sequence and channel dimensions, processes the information through a configurable non-linear module and subsequently reconstructs the forecasts.\nL2320: Moreover, Li-Net integrates a sparse Top-K Softmax attention mechanism within a multi-scale projection framework to address these challenges. A core innovation is its ability to seamlessly incorporate and fuse multi-modal embeddings, guiding the sparse attention process to focus on the most informative time steps and feature channels.\nL2321: Through the experiment results on multiple real-world benchmark datasets demonstrate that Li-Net achieves competitive performance compared to state-of-the-art baseline methods. Furthermore, Li-Net provides a superior balance between prediction accuracy and computational burden, exhibiting significantly lower memory usage and faster inference times. Detailed ablation studies and parameter sensitivity analyses validate the effectiveness of each key component in our proposed architecture.\nL2322: Keywords: Multivariate Time Series Forecasting, Sparse Attention Mechanism, Multimodal Information Fusion, Non-linear relationship\nL2323: [182] cite1488†arXiv:2604.00445 (replaced) [cite1489†pdf , cite1490†html , cite1491†other ]\nL2324: \nL2325: \nL2326: Title: Towards Reliable Truth-Aligned Uncertainty Estimation in Large Language Models\nL2327: \nL2328: cite1343†Ponhvoan Srey , cite1492†Quang Minh Nguyen , cite517†Xiaobao Wu , cite1346†Anh Tuan Luu L2329: \nL2330: Comments: Accepted to EMNLP Findings 2026\nL2331: \nL2332: Subjects: Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Computation and Language (cs.CL)\nL2333: Uncertainty estimation (UE) aims to quantify uncertainty in large language model (LLM) outputs to flag potential hallucinations and support reliable deployment. However, UE metrics often exhibit unstable performance across models and datasets, limiting their practical utility. We formalise this phenomenon as proxy failure, arising because UE metrics are derived from model behaviour rather than being explicitly grounded in factual correctness.\nL2334: We show that when mutual information between a UE score and correctness is low, the score's discriminability must be close to chance. To address this, we propose Truth AnChoring (TAC), a lightweight post-hoc calibration method that maps raw UE scores to truth-aligned correctness probabilities. Even with noisy and few-shot supervision, TAC continues to improve calibration, providing a practical calibration protocol.\nL2335: Our findings highlight the limitations of treating heuristic UE metrics as direct indicators of uncertainty about factual correctness, and position our TAC as a principled step toward more reliable uncertainty estimation for LLMs. The code repository is available at cite1493†this https URL†github.com .\nL2336: [183] cite1494†arXiv:2604.01151 (replaced) [cite1495†pdf , cite1496†html , cite1497†other ]\nL2337: \nL2338: \nL2339: Title: Detecting Multi-Agent Collusion Through Multi-Agent Interpretability\nL2340: \nL2341: cite1498†Aaron Rose , cite1499†Carissa Cullen , cite1500†Sahar Abdelnabi , cite1501†Philip Torr , cite1502†Brandon Gary Kaplowitz , cite1503†Christian Schroeder de Witt L2342: \nL2343: Subjects: Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Machine Learning (cs.LG); Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)\nL2344: Non-verbalised or secret collusion may impose a ceiling on transcript-level monitoring in multi-agent deployments. Internal model activations are known to provide an additional monitoring surface for single-agent phenomena such as deception, but detecting collusion within an unknown subgroup is ultimately a joint inference task over multiple agents.\nL2345: This paper derives collusion detection statistics based on the composite hypothesis testing literature and introduces HELIOS, a multi-agent detection framework for collusion. Using per-agent activation probes as sensors, we then evaluate these principled statistics alongside heuristics on NARCBench, a novel benchmark for evaluating multi-agent collusion detection under environment distribution shift.\nL2346: Across four open-weight models, both our group-level statistics and heuristics transfer zero-shot to NARCBenchs holdout environments. Notably, the best method on every model reaches 0.99 to 1.00 AUROC when transferred from committee deliberations to a steganographic collusion task that unprimed transcript monitors fail to separate.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nComputation and Language (https://arxiv.org/list/cs.CL/new)\nciteturn41254view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/list/cs.CL/new\",\"lineno\":1900}); Total lines: 2886\nL1875: Due to privacy restrictions, there's a shortage of publicly available speech recognition datasets in the medical domain. In this work, we present VietMed - a Vietnamese speech recognition dataset in the medical domain comprising 16h of labeled medical speech, 1000h of unlabeled medical speech and 1200h of unlabeled general-domain speech.\nL1876: To our best knowledge, VietMed is by far the world's largest public medical speech recognition dataset in 7 aspects: total duration, number of speakers, diseases, recording conditions, speaker roles, unique medical terms and accents. VietMed is also by far the largest public Vietnamese speech dataset in terms of total duration. Additionally, we are the first to present a medical ASR dataset covering all ICD-10 disease groups and all accents within a country.\nL1877: Moreover, we release the first public large-scale pre-trained models for Vietnamese ASR, w2v2-Viet and XLSR-53-Viet, along with the first public large-scale fine-tuned models for medical ASR. Even without any medical data in unsupervised pre-training, our best pre-trained model XLSR-53-Viet generalizes very well to the medical domain by outperforming state-of-the-art XLSR-53, from 51.8% to 29.6% WER on test set (a relative reduction of more than 40%).\nL1878: All code, data and models are made publicly available: cite1263†this https URL†github.com .\nL1879: [148] cite1264†arXiv:2502.14932 (replaced) [cite1265†pdf , cite1266†html , cite1267†other ]\nL1880: \nL1881: \nL1882: Title: Learning to Retrieve and Reason on Knowledge Graph through Active Self-Reflection\nL1883: \nL1884: cite870†Han Zhang , cite1268†Langshi Zhou , cite1269†Hanfang Yang L1885: \nL1886: Comments: 10 pages, 3 figures, 5 tables. Accepted at the LASS Workshop at CIKM 2026\nL1887: \nL1888: Subjects: Computation and Language (cs.CL)\nL1889: Extensive research has investigated the integration of large language models (LLMs) with knowledge graphs to enhance the reasoning process. However, understanding how models perform reasoning utilizing structured graph knowledge remains underexplored. Most existing approaches rely on LLMs or retrievers to make binary judgments regarding the utilization of knowledge, which is too coarse. Meanwhile, there is still a lack of feedback mechanisms for reflection and control throughout the entire reasoning path.\nL1890: This paper proposes an Active self-Reflection framework for knowledge Graph reasoning (ArG), introducing for the first time an end-to-end training approach to achieve iterative reasoning grounded on structured graphs. Within the framework, the model leverages special tokens to actively control the reasoning process, achieving on-demand retrieval and multi-dimensional, finer-grained knowledge evaluation.\nL1891: The reasoning paths generated by the model exhibit high interpretability, enabling deeper exploration of the model's understanding of structured knowledge. Ultimately, the proposed model achieves outstanding results compared to existing baselines in knowledge graph reasoning tasks.\nL1892: [149] cite1270†arXiv:2502.15092 (replaced) [cite1271†pdf , cite1272†html , cite1273†other ]\nL1893: \nL1894: \nL1895: Title: Optimizing Singular Spectrum for Large Language Model Compression\nL1896: \nL1897: cite1274†Dengjie Li , cite1275†Tiancheng Shen , cite852†Yao Zhou , cite1276†Bernard Ghanem , cite179†Yibo Yang , cite1277†Ming-Hsuan Yang L1898: \nL1899: Subjects: Computation and Language (cs.CL)\nL1900: Large language models (LLMs) have demonstrated remarkable capabilities, yet prohibitive parameter complexity often hinders their deployment. Existing singular value decomposition (SVD) based compression methods simply deem singular values as importance scores of decomposed components. However, this importance ordered by singular values does not necessarily correlate with the performance of a downstream task.\nL1901: In this work, we introduce SoCo (Singular spectrum optimization for large language model Compression), a novel compression framework that learns to rescale the decomposed components of SVD in a data-driven manner.\nL1902: Concretely, we employ a learnable diagonal matrix to assign importance scores for singular spectrum and develop a three-stage training process that progressively refines these scores from initial coarse compression to fine-grained sparsification-thereby striking an effective balance between aggressive model compression and performance preservation.\nL1903: Thanks to the learnable singular spectrum, SoCo adaptively prunes components according to the sparsified importance scores, rather than relying on the fixed order of singular values. More importantly, the remaining components with amplified importance scores can compensate for the loss of the pruned ones. Experimental evaluations across multiple LLMs and benchmarks demonstrate that SoCo surpasses the state-of-the-art methods in model compression.\nL1904: [150] cite1278†arXiv:2505.09039 (replaced) [cite1279†pdf , cite1280†html , cite1281†other ]\nL1905: \nL1906: \nL1907: Title: Atomic Consistency Preference Optimization for Long-Form Question Answering\nL1908: \nL1909: cite1057†Jingfeng Chen , cite1282†Raghuveer Thirukovalluru , cite890†Junlin Wang , cite1283†Kaiwei Luo , cite1284†Bhuwan Dhingra L1910: \nL1911: Comments: 13 pages, 1 figure\nL1912: \nL1913: Subjects: Computation and Language (cs.CL)\nL1914: Large Language Models (LLMs) often produce factoid hallucinations - plausible yet incorrect answers. A common mitigation strategy is model alignment, which improves factual accuracy by training on curated (factual, non-factual) pairs. However, this approach often relies on a stronger model (e.g., GPT-4) or an external knowledge base to assess factual correctness that may not always be accessible.\nL1915: Addressing this, we propose Atomic Consistency Preference Optimization (ACPO), a self-supervised preference-tuning method that enhances factual accuracy without external supervision. ACPO leverages atomic consistency signals (i.e., the agreement of individual facts across multiple stochastic responses) to identify high- and low-quality data pairs for model alignment.\nL1916: Despite being fully self-supervised, ACPO outperforms the strong supervised alignment baseline by 1.95 points averaged across Phi-3 and Llama3 on the LongFact and BioGen datasets, demonstrating its effectiveness in improving factual reliability without relying on external models or knowledge bases.\nL1917: [151] cite1285†arXiv:2505.18588 (replaced) [cite1286†pdf , cite1287†html , cite1288†other ]\nL1918: \nL1919: \nL1920: Title: Safety Alignment via Constrained Knowledge Unlearning\nL1921: \nL1922: cite382†Zesheng Shi , cite852†Yucheng Zhou , cite254†Jing Li L1923: \nL1924: Subjects: Computation and Language (cs.CL); Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\nL1925: Despite significant progress in safety alignment, large language models (LLMs) remain susceptible to jailbreak attacks. Existing defense mechanisms have not fully deleted harmful knowledge in LLMs, which allows such attacks to bypass safeguards and produce harmful outputs. To address this challenge, we propose a novel safety alignment strategy, Constrained Knowledge Unlearning (CKU), which focuses on two primary objectives: knowledge localization and retention, and unlearning harmful knowledge.\nL1926: CKU works by scoring neurons in specific multilayer perceptron (MLP) layers to identify a subset U of neurons associated with useful knowledge. During the unlearning process, CKU prunes the gradients of neurons in U to preserve valuable knowledge while effectively mitigating harmful content. Experimental results demonstrate that CKU significantly enhances model safety without compromising overall performance, offering a superior balance between safety and utility compared to existing methods.\nL1927: Additionally, our analysis of neuron knowledge sensitivity across various MLP layers provides valuable insights into the mechanics of safety alignment and model knowledge editing.\nL1928: [152] cite1289†arXiv:2507.20055 (replaced) [cite1290†pdf , cite1291†html , cite1292†other ]\nL1929: \nL1930: \nL1931: Title: Automatic Tensor-level Execution of Abstract Interpreters for Neural Networks\nL1932: \nL1933: cite1293†Avaljot Singh , cite1294†Yasmin Chandini Sarita , cite1295†Aditya Mishra , cite1296†Gagandeep Singh , cite1297†Charith Mendis L1934: \nL1935: Subjects: Computation and Language (cs.CL)\nL1936: Developing new abstract interpretation-based DNN certifiers is difficult because their mathematical design is expressed at the neuron level, while implementations must operate at the tensor level. Existing libraries such as auto_LiRPA, IVAN, and ERAN support fixed sets of certifiers; extending them requires expertise in formal methods and tensor programming. Domain-specific languages such as ConstraintFlow let researchers specify certifiers at the neuron level, but cannot run them on real DNNs.\nL1937: We present Neuton, a framework that automatically translates neuron-level certifier specifications into tensor-level executables, enabling new DNN certifiers without manual tensor programming. Neuton makes three contributions. First, a stack-based intermediate representation (IR) and shape analysis infer implicit tensor operations, including broadcasts, dimension expansions, and reductions, required for correct execution.\nL1938: Second, domain-specific rewrite rules optimize common tensor operation sequences into more efficient equivalents. Third, gBCSR is a sparse tensor format using variable-sized, layer-aligned blocks over a non-zero default value, tailored to DNN certifier sparsity.\nL1939: We prove that translation preserves neuron-level semantics. We evaluate Neuton on 8 certifiers and 32 DNN benchmarks. Neuton compiles four existing certifiers and produces the first executable implementations of four others: two previously specified but never run, and two new designs. These expose precision-runtime trade-offs unavailable in existing libraries. For example, SkipPoly matches DeepPoly's precision on all 32 DNNs while running as fast or faster on half.\nL1940: Neuton's runtime on existing certifiers is competitive with hand-optimized libraries. Code: cite1298†this https URL†github.com L1941: [153] cite1299†arXiv:2508.17771 (replaced) [cite1300†pdf , cite1301†html , cite1302†other ]\nL1942: \nL1943: \nL1944: Title: Speculating LLMs' Chinese Training Data Pollution from Their Tokens\nL1945: \nL1946: cite1303†Qingjie Zhang , cite322†Di Wang , cite1304†Haoting Qian , cite1305†Liu Yan , cite1306†Tianwei Zhang , cite1307†Ke Xu , cite1308†Qi Li , cite1309†Minlie Huang , cite39†Hewu Li , cite1310†Han Qiu L1947: \nL1948: Subjects: Computation and Language (cs.CL)\nL1949: Tokens are basic elements in the datasets for LLM training. It is well-known that many tokens representing Chinese phrases in the vocabulary of GPT (4o/4o-mini/o1/o3/4.5/4.1/o4-mini) are indicating contents like pornography or online gambling. Based on this observation, our goal is to locate Polluted Chinese (PoC) tokens in LLMs and study the relationship between PoC tokens' existence and training data. (1) We give a formal definition and taxonomy of PoC tokens based on the GPT's vocabulary.\n"
